#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151849 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/id #uuid "b2dd8c4b-cabe-5a47-b6cd-b426f41280a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14603" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$, defined by $x \\sim y$ if $12 \\, \\mid \\, (x-y)$. Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[9]$?\n\n1. $9$\n2. $21$\n3. $22$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[9]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $9$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[9] &= {x ∈ Z: x∼9} \\\\\n&= {x ∈ Z: 12 \\mid (x - 9)} \\\\\n&= {x ∈ Z: x = 9 + 12k, k ∈ Z} \\\\\n&= {…,-15,-3, 9, 21, 33, …}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $9$ and $21$ are in $[9]$, while $22$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151851 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/id #uuid "bc24cde3-b907-5bc0-9889-a7a54b10a2a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14605" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, defined by $x \\sim y$ if $\\sin x = \\sin y$. Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$?\n\n1. $\\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n2. $\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n3. $\\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in [x]\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\sim x\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) &= \\sin x \\\\\n1 &= \\sin x \\\\\n\\sin x &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin \\left(-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1 \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151852 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21e3832f-b11f-5501-bf39-74665b88c38b" :knowledge-point/title "Counting Equivalence Classes"}] :question/id #uuid "1e93cc7e-4adc-5a6a-94d1-966d67511a19" :question/math-academy-id "e-14606" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on the set $A = \\{0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}$, defined by $a \\sim b$ if $\\textrm{i}^a = \\textrm{i}^b$, where $\\textrm{i}$ is the imaginary unit in $\\mathbb{C}$. How many distinct equivalence classes does $\\sim$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $\\sim$ partition $A$, meaning that any element $a \\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0] &= {a ∈ A∣a∼0} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}^{0}} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = 1} \\\\\n&= {0, 4, 8} \\\\\n[1] &= {a ∈ A∣a∼1} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}^{1}} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}} \\\\\n&= {1, 5, 9} \\\\\n[2] &= {a ∈ A∣a∼2} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}^{2}} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} =-1} \\\\\n&= {2, 6} \\\\\n[3] &= {a ∈ A∣a∼3} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} = \\textrm{i}^{3}} \\\\\n&= {a ∈ A∣\\textrm{i}^{a} =-\\textrm{i}} \\\\\n&= {3, 7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach element of $A$ has been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $4$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0] = \\{0, 4, 8 \\}, \\quad [1] = \\{1, 5, 9 \\}, \\quad [2] = \\{2, 6 \\}, \\quad [3] = \\{3, 7 \\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceda99fe-9a89-538b-92fe-b9c3561af0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151032} {:answer/id #uuid "d631c338-a791-53b2-91c3-3159ad3deab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151033} {:answer/id #uuid "2c2719e4-e6df-5dc9-8684-b89dfbd92824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151034} {:answer/id #uuid "47f6ca37-dd85-59da-bfe2-4f7a859f36ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151035} {:answer/id #uuid "6979a5a8-b5a8-55b8-9ed4-6ea49cd616c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151036}] :answer-field/id #uuid "28538709-e3ec-5163-8146-0112145cee55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151031}] :question/id #uuid "57bb64c1-83a8-5ad6-a9bc-3ab2aa9fabbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-76893" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C$, defined by $x∼y$ if $x^{4} = y^{4}$. Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $\\textrm{i}$?\n\n1. $[- 1]$\n2. $[- \\textrm{i}]$\n3. $[2\\textrm{i}]$"}]}
